data.content:
Done. Here's what was added:

**`Clock.cs`** — a zero-dependency ambient clock using `AsyncLocal<T>` so parallel tests don't interfere.

**Usage in production code** (already applied to `InvoiceCalculator`):
```csharp
Clock.UtcNow   // instead of DateTimeOffset.UtcNow
```

**Usage in tests:**
```csharp
var due = new DateTimeOffset(2026, 1, 1, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero);
using (Clock.Override(due.AddDays(-1)))
{
    Assert.False(new InvoiceCalculator().IsOverdue(due));
}
using (Clock.Override(due.AddDays(1)))
{
    Assert.True(new InvoiceCalculator().IsOverdue(due));
}
```

**Why this pattern:**
- No DI container needed — fits a plain class library.
- `AsyncLocal` means concurrent/parallel tests each get their own clock value.
- Production overhead is a single null-check per call.
- `IDisposable` scope ensures cleanup even if a test throws.

For any other classes reading `DateTimeOffset.UtcNow`, just swap them to `Clock.UtcNow`.
